{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClassCrossTerm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClassCrossTerm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovTraceInterleavedCrossTerm","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.cyclicSquare_cross_term_neg_convD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.cyclicSquare_cross_term_neg_convD","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L207-L218","doc":"At convention D the three-strand cross term is `−(q⁴ + q² + 1)/(q² + 1)²` — the Markov\ndefect itself (`markovDefect_convD`) — and it is **negative** for every real `q > 0`: the\ncyclic square's trace is smaller than the Hopf chain's, so a cyclically braided triple carries\nextra log-suppression, i.e. extra binding. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.interleaved_eq_stab_cyclicSquare","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.interleaved_eq_stab_cyclicSquare","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L188-L203","doc":"**The `n = 4` interleaved word is the stabilised cyclic square.** `tr(g₀ g₁ g₀ g₂ g₁) =\nz · tr((g₁ g₀)²)`: rotate `g₁` to the front, peel `g₂`. So the `n = 4` cross term\n`z (z² − h z − 1)` is the three-strand one times a Markov stabilisation — the object behind\nthe cross term is the torus knot `T(3,2)` of the cyclically braided triple, not anything\nfour-strand. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.cyclicSquare_cross_term","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.cyclicSquare_cross_term","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L174-L186","doc":"**THE MINIMAL CROSS TERM — three strands.** With the same content `(2, 2)`, the cyclic\nsquare `(gₘ gₘ₊₁)²` and the Hopf chain `gₘ² gₘ₊₁²` differ by exactly the convention-D Markov\ndefect: `tr(x (gₘ gₘ₊₁)²) − tr(x gₘ² gₘ₊₁²) = (z² − h z − 1) · tr x`. This is the smallest\ninstance where the product formula fails — `n = 3`, not `n = 4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.tr_cyclicSquare","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.tr_cyclicSquare","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L132-L172","doc":"The cyclic-square trace with prefix: `tr(x gₘ gₘ₊₁ gₘ gₘ₊₁) = (h z (h z + 1) + z²) · tr x`,\nvia the braid relation and the quadratic. Requires `x` to commute with `gₘ₊₁` (far\ncommutation of the prefix with the new generator). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.tr_hopfChain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.tr_hopfChain","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L117-L130","doc":"The Hopf-chain trace with prefix: `tr(x gₘ² gₘ₊₁²) = (hz+1)² · tr x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.twoStepClimbRevisit_eq_monotone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.twoStepClimbRevisit_eq_monotone","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L99-L113","doc":"**Climbing two steps and returning carries no cross term.** `x gₘ gₘ₊₁ gₘ₊₂ gₘ`: move\n`gₘ` past `gₘ₊₂` (far commutation), peel `gₘ₊₂` (Markov), then the one-step identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.skipRevisit_eq_monotone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.skipRevisit_eq_monotone","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L92-L97","doc":"**Skipping the neighbour carries no cross term.** If `gₘ₊₂` commutes with `gₘ`,\n`x gₘ gₘ₊₂ gₘ = x gₘ gₘ gₘ₊₂` outright. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.oneStepRevisit_eq_monotone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RevisitClass.oneStepRevisit_eq_monotone","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RevisitClassCrossTerm.lean#L76-L90","doc":"**One-step revisit carries no cross term.** For any prefix `x ∈ H m`,\n`tr(x gₘ gₘ₊₁ gₘ) = tr(x gₘ gₘ gₘ₊₁)`: rotate the trailing `gₘ` to the front, peel `gₘ₊₁`,\nrotate back. Cyclicity and one Markov move only — no braid relation, no quadratic. This is\n`qou-ls86`'s `n = 3` identity with an arbitrary prefix. ","signatureChunk":0}]}